===ECC GPG Keys===

Application number: 828366'

The derivation path format is: <code>m/83696968'/828366'/{key_type}'/{key_bits}'/{key_index}'</code>

All path components are hardened (denoted by <code>'</code>). The key_type values and
their corresponding curves are:

{| class="wikitable"
! key_type
! OpenPGP Key Type
! key_bits
! Algorithm / Curve
|-
| 0
| ECC(Brainpool)
| 256, 384, 512
| brainpoolP256r1, brainpoolP384r1, brainpoolP512r1 respectively
|-
| 1
| ECC(Curve25519)
| 256
| EdDSALegacy (certify/sign/auth), Curve25519Legacy (encrypt); OpenPGP v4 packets
|-
| 2
| ECC(secp256k1)
| 256
| secp256k1
|-
| 3
| ECC(NIST)
| 256, 384, 521
| NIST P-256, P-384, P-521 respectively
|-
| 4
| ECC(Curve25519)
| 256
| Ed25519 (certify/sign/auth), X25519 (encrypt); OpenPGP v6 packets
|}

Valid values for each derivation path component are:

* <code>key_type</code>: 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4.
* <code>key_bits</code>: one of the values listed for the key_type in the table above
  (256, 384 or 512 for key_type 0; 256 for key_types 1, 2 and 4; 256, 384 or 521 for
  key_type 3).
* <code>key_index</code>: any hardened index, 0 to 2<sup>31</sup>-1.
* <code>sub_key</code>: 0 (ENCRYPT), 1 (AUTHENTICATE) or 2 (SIGN).

Paths with any other value are invalid and MUST be rejected; new key types or key sizes
can only be added by a revision of this specification.

key_types 0 through 3 produce OpenPGP version 4 keys: RFC 4880 packets using the ECC
algorithms as updated by RFC 9580. key_type 4 produces OpenPGP version 6 keys as specified
in RFC 9580. The derivation scheme below is identical for both; the key_types differ only
in the OpenPGP packet format and in the Curve25519 algorithm identifiers.

<b>Private Key Derivation</b>

All ECC GPG keys are derived from the 64 bytes of BIP85 entropy produced for the path. The
entropy seeds BIP85-DRNG-SHAKE256, and every byte consumed by the steps below is read from
that stream.

For the Weierstrass curves (key_types 0, 2 and 3) the private scalar is generated with
the extra-random-bits method of FIPS 186-5 section A.2.1: with <code>n</code> the order
of the curve and <code>N = ceil(log2(n))</code>, read <code>L = ceil((N + 64) / 8)</code>
bytes from the DRNG (40, 56, 72 or 74 bytes), interpret them as a big-endian integer
<code>c</code>, and compute <code>d = (c mod (n - 1)) + 1</code>. This specification
selects N + 64 bits, rounded up to whole bytes, for the A.2.1 modular-reduction method;
the extra bits keep the bias of the modular reduction below 2^-64, and no candidate is
ever rejected, so a single DRNG read always yields a valid scalar in [1, n - 1]. The
scalar d is the private key, serialized as a big-endian byte string of the curve's base
length (32, 48, 64 or 66 octets).

For Curve25519 (key_types 1 and 4) the private key is the first 32 bytes read from the
DRNG, used as-is: as the Ed25519 private seed of RFC 8032 for CERTIFY, SIGN and
AUTHENTICATE keys, and as the X25519 private key of RFC 7748 for ENCRYPT keys. The seed is
not clamped or hashed by the derivation; the algorithms hash or clamp it internally.

<b>OpenPGP Key Material</b>

The algorithm-specific public key material inside the key packets is:

* ECDSA (algorithm 19) for the certify/sign/auth keys of key_types 0, 2 and 3: the curve
  OID followed by an MPI of the uncompressed SEC1 point <code>0x04||X||Y</code>
  (RFC 9580 section 5.5.5.4).
* ECDH (algorithm 18) for the encrypt subkeys of key_types 0, 2 and 3: the curve OID, an
  MPI of the uncompressed SEC1 point, and the KDF parameter field described below
  (RFC 9580 section 5.5.5.6).
* EdDSA (algorithm 22, EdDSALegacy) for the key_type 1 certify/sign/auth keys: the Ed25519
  OID followed by an MPI of <code>0x40||native point</code> (RFC 9580 section 5.5.5.5).
* ECDH (algorithm 18, Curve25519Legacy) for the key_type 1 encrypt subkey: the Curve25519
  OID, an MPI of <code>0x40||native point</code>, and the KDF parameter field described
  below (RFC 9580 section 5.5.5.6).
* The native v6 algorithms for key_type 4: Ed25519 (algorithm 27) and X25519 (algorithm
  25). Their public key material is the raw 32-octet native public key, with no OID, MPI
  framing or KDF parameters (RFC 9580 sections 5.5.5.9 and 5.5.5.7).

Curve OIDs and native point encodings are as defined in RFC 9580 sections 9.2 and 9.2.1.
secp256k1 is not listed in the RFC 9580 curve registry; key_type 2 uses its SEC 2 OID
<code>1.3.132.0.10</code> (octets <code>2B 81 04 00 0A</code>). Because the curve is outside
the registry, support for secp256k1 ECDSA and ECDH keys varies between OpenPGP
implementations, and implementations limited to RFC 9580 may reject such keys entirely.
Users should confirm that their intended tools support both signing and encryption with
key_type 2 before relying on it.
The ECDH KDF parameter field (RFC 9580 section 5.5.5.6) is the four octets
<code>0x03 0x01 {hash} {sym}</code>, where the hash and symmetric algorithm IDs are:

