When a key is exported as a transferable secret key, the secret material is encoded per
RFC 9580 sections 5.5.5.4, 5.5.5.5 and 5.5.5.6.1: the EdDSALegacy secret is the seed
itself; the Curve25519Legacy secret is the reversed clamped scalar stored as a 255-bit
MPI; all other curves store the scalar as a plain big-endian MPI. Subkeys are bound to the
primary key with the usual self-signatures and binding signatures (RFC 9580 section 5.2.3).
Native v6 Ed25519 and X25519 secret material is encoded as the original 32 octets,
without MPI framing (RFC 9580 sections 5.5.5.9 and 5.5.5.7).

Determinism applies to derived key material and fingerprints, not to the complete
transferable key. v6 signatures carry a salt (RFC 9580 section 5.2.3), which RFC 9580
section 13.2 describes as random; this specification requires exporters to generate a fresh
random salt for each v6 signature and never to derive it from the key material, path or
fingerprint. Exported v6 keys therefore differ between runs while their key material and
fingerprints stay the same. ASCII-armored v6 keys MUST omit the CRC24 footer (RFC 9580
section 6.1).

<b>Primary Keys and Subkeys</b>

The primary/subkey scheme follows the same structure as [[#RSA GPG|RSA GPG]], using this
application's derivation path:

* Primary key: <code>m/83696968'/828366'/{key_type}'/{key_bits}'/{key_index}'</code>
* Sub keys:  <code>m/83696968'/828366'/{key_type}'/{key_bits}'/{key_index}'/{sub_key}'</code>

Splitting the keyring this way allows the CERTIFY-capable primary key to remain offline
while the ENCRYPT, AUTHENTICATE and SIGN subkeys are used day to day.

As with the existing RSA GPG derivation scheme, this application defines one subkey per
role for each primary key. Subkey replacement and rotation are outside the scope of this
specification. Reusing a path regenerates the same key; incrementing key_index changes
the primary key and its entire subkey set. The sub_key component selects a role, not a
generation, and MUST NOT be incremented beyond 2 to request replacement keys. Additional
path components for subkey generations are not defined and MUST be rejected. OpenPGP
revocation and binding of independently generated replacement keys are separate operations
and do not extend this derivation scheme.

All subkeys use the same key_type and key_bits as their primary key. Mixing key types
between a primary key and its subkeys is out of scope for this specification.

For key_type 1 (Curve25519), the OpenPGP algorithm used depends on the key capability,
because Curve25519 takes different forms for signing versus encryption in the OpenPGP
standard:
* CERTIFY, SIGN, and AUTHENTICATE capabilities use Ed25519 (EdDSA)
* ENCRYPT capability uses X25519 (ECDH)

This means the primary key and sub_keys 1 (auth) and 2 (sign) under key_type 1 produce
Ed25519 keys, while sub_key 0 (encrypt) produces an X25519 key. The same derived entropy
bytes are interpreted according to the required algorithm. The same holds for key_type 4,
which uses the native v6 Ed25519 and X25519 algorithms instead of the legacy ones.

<b>Note on timestamps:</b>
See [[#RSA GPG|RSA GPG]] for the fixed creation date requirement and faketime guidance.
Because the key fingerprint covers the creation time, the creation time of every primary
and subkey packet MUST be the fixed UNIX Epoch timestamp 1231006505.

<b>Note on GPG key capabilities on smartcard/hardware devices:</b>
See [[#RSA GPG|RSA GPG]] for smart-card slot loading guidance.

<b>Fingerprints:</b> v4 key fingerprints are SHA-1 hashes of the v4 public key packet as
specified in RFC 4880; v6 key fingerprints are SHA-256 hashes of the v6 public key packet
as specified in RFC 9580 section 5.5.4.3.

